<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.accounts-keepwarm — keep the shared account cache inside its TTL, so the interactive
  launch path finds something to serve instead of abstaining.

  WHY IT EXISTS. `claude` routes with `--route interactive --max-wait 0 --max-age 600`: it may
  serve the cache or abstain, and may never sweep, take the single-flight lock, or heal — because
  the sweeper's documented worst case is MINUTES and its floor behind a wedged holder is 240s then
  exit 5, and none of that may ever sit in front of an operator pressing Enter. The cost of that
  guarantee is that when the cache is cold the router abstains and the launcher falls back to the
  pinned account. This job keeps the cache warm so abstention is rare rather than routine.

  WHY IT LIVES IN launchd/staged/ AND NOT launchd/. A plist in launchd/ is COPIED to
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents by install.sh, and macOS loads agents there at the next login — so
  `expect = staged` in fleet.manifest suppresses `launchctl bootstrap` NOW but does not stop the
  copy from arming the job at next login. launchd/staged/ is outside install.sh's non-recursive
  glob, so this job cannot start until the operator deliberately installs it.

  StartInterval 180 since 2026-08-11 (was 60), and the arithmetic below is now a RECORD of why 60
  failed rather than a live derivation. cache_ttl_s is 90 (accounts.json). A warm sweep measures
  1.85-2.53s in the FOREGROUND, and that is NOT what this job gets: ProcessType Background +
  LowPriorityIO clamp it to PRI 4, and the first 6 live runs measured 6.2-11.8s — 3-5x the bench.
  60 was sized off that band: worst-case age 60 + 11.8 = 71.8s, an 18.2s margin inside the TTL.

  🚨 THAT BAND WAS ITSELF A NON-VERDICT — n=6, on the first six runs, before the fleet reached its
  real load. Re-measured 2026-08-11 over 25 consecutive live ticks: 3.9-36.8s, i.e. the upper bound
  is 3.1x the one the interval was sized from. At 36.8s the worst-case age is 60 + 36.8 = 96.8s,
  which EXCEEDS the 90s TTL — so the job had silently stopped meeting the invariant it exists for,
  and no surface said so. Note the shape of the error, because it is the same one twice: a bound
  sized from a foreground bench is a non-verdict once Background taxes it, and a bound sized from
  SIX Background runs is a non-verdict once the box is loaded. Both times the fix was to re-measure
  the production band, never to re-reason from the previous one.

  AND THE TWO CONSTRAINTS ARE IRRECONCILABLE AT TTL 90, which is the finding that actually forced
  this change. Meeting the TTL with a 36.8s sweep needs StartInterval < 53s; staying outside the
  ~90s endpoint throttle needs StartInterval > 90s. There is no value satisfying both, so one had
  to give — and the block below already ruled which: the margin is a QUALITY knob and the throttle
  is a CORRECTNESS one. 180 puts the poll interval at 2x the throttle window.

  THAT MARGIN IS A QUALITY KNOB, NOT A SAFETY ONE. If a consumer does miss the TTL, the interactive
  launcher abstains and falls back to its PINNED account — the status quo, not a failure. So the
  correct response to a shrinking margin is to lengthen StartInterval (fewer sweeps, more
  abstentions) rather than to shorten it (more sweeps, more 429 exposure per the risk below). 75s was rejected: it leaves 12.5s, which is less than the variance ONE fetch_usage
  retry introduces (+1s sleep, up to a 12s timeout), so a single retried account would put the
  producer over the TTL and hand the miss to a consumer. Fleet precedent for 60s:
  com.claude.session-search-sweep and com.claude.capacity-alarm.

  --max-age 90 (was 30). REFRESH-AHEAD IS RETIRED, deliberately, and this is the paragraph the
  measurement overturned. The old reasoning — refresh at age>30 so a consumer never meets an expired
  cache — was sound in isolation and had one unstated consequence: with --max-age at interval/2, the
  cache age at every tick is ~interval, i.e. ALWAYS past the bound, so the producer swept on 100% of
  ticks. Confirmed in its own out.log: 25 of 25 consecutive ticks logged `swept`, never once
  `served-cache`, with age_s=0 every time. That is a poll every 60s per account against an endpoint
  whose throttle window is ~90s — the tool's own /accounts doc says "never poll tighter than the 90s
  TTL", and this job was the only thing on the box violating it.
  At --max-age 90 the flag is refresh-ON-expiry: with StartInterval 180 the tick always sweeps
  anyway, so the value now documents intent (sweep once the cache is genuinely past TTL) rather than
  driving the cadence. The cadence is StartInterval, and it is the only knob that ever was.

  IT ALSO PRODUCES THE SESSIONSTART BOARD (W3.6, 2026-08-11), and this job is the ONLY thing that
  does. `--keepwarm` renders `--readout --narrow` to board_file (default
  /tmp/claude-accounts-board.txt) in the same pass, and hooks/accounts-board.sh does nothing but
  read that file. So if the startup board reads STALE or "unavailable", the fault is HERE — check
  this job before anything else (`launchctl list | grep accounts-keepwarm`, then the out.log below;
  the line reports `board=<path>` or `board=FAILED` every tick). The hook cannot self-heal by
  fetching live: `claude-accounts --readout` measures 5.33s against a 5s hook timeout, which is the
  whole reason the work is on this job instead. Rendering costs ~3ms on a served cache, so it does
  not move any figure above.

  IT SWEEPS WITH HEAL, DELIBERATELY. cache_read refuses a no-heal payload to a heal-wanting caller
  whenever any row is BAD_AUTH, so a --no-heal producer would poison the cache for every other
  consumer precisely on the day an account is logged out — the day it matters most.

  THE RISK THIS CARRIES, NAMED. ~1.4k sweeps/day against the OAuth usage endpoint. A 429 is classed
  as a transient poll-throttle and sets row["error"], an errored row reads as data-unavailable, and
  cc-route turns that into exit 3 = fire-blind refusal for DISPATCHED work. So a misconfigured or
  over-frequent producer could degrade the fleet in a way that looks nothing like a latency feature.
  If throttling appears, lengthen StartInterval before anything else, and read
  ~/.claude/logs/claude-accounts.log — the 429 branch is instrumented.

  🚨 THAT RISK CAME TRUE ON 2026-08-11, and this note is the record. Symptom as the operator met it:
  bare `claude` routed to an account the /accounts table did not recommend. Cause: 3 of 4 accounts
  carried `poll throttled ↻ (cached usage)`, an errored row is excluded from routing, so the desk
  lane picked the only survivor — correctly, from a one-account eligible set. Which account survived
  varied per sweep, so the pick walked (next -> next2 -> next4 inside 40 minutes), and once a session
  landed on one the `sticky` rung held it there. Nothing in the table marked the excluded rows, so
  four healthy-looking accounts sat beside a pick that ignored three of them.
  The remedy applied was this paragraph's own: lengthen StartInterval, 60 -> 180.

  Kill: launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.claude.accounts-keepwarm
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.claude.accounts-keepwarm</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; exec "$HOME/.claude/bin/claude-accounts" --keepwarm --max-age 90</string>
  </array>

  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>180</integer>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <false/>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>

  <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
  <true/>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/accounts-keepwarm.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/accounts-keepwarm.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
